### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a custom annotation processor in Java. The annotation processor should process classes annotated with a custom annotation and generate a report containing information about the annotated classes.

Create a custom annotation called `@CustomAnnotation` that can be applied to classes. The annotation should have a single attribute called `description` of type String.

Your annotation processor should scan the classes in a given package and generate a report containing the following information for each class annotated with `@CustomAnnotation`:
1. Fully qualified class name
2. Package name
3. Description provided in the annotation

Your task is to implement the annotation processor to achieve this functionality.

### Solution:
```java
import javax.annotation.processing.*;
import javax.lang.model.SourceVersion;
import javax.lang.model.element.Element;
import javax.lang.model.element.TypeElement;
import javax.tools.Diagnostic;
import javax.tools.JavaFileObject;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.Set;

@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.CustomAnnotation")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_8)
public class CustomAnnotationProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {

    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(CustomAnnotation.class)) {
            if (element instanceof TypeElement) {
                TypeElement typeElement = (TypeElement) element;
                String packageName = processingEnv.getElementUtils().getPackageOf(typeElement).getQualifiedName().toString();
                String className = typeElement.getQualifiedName().toString();
                String description = typeElement.getAnnotation(CustomAnnotation.class).description();

                generateReport(packageName, className, description);
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private void generateReport(String packageName, String className, String description) {
        try {
            JavaFileObject fileObject = processingEnv.getFiler().createResource(StandardLocation.SOURCE_OUTPUT, "", "report.txt");
            try (PrintWriter writer = new PrintWriter(fileObject.openWriter())) {
                writer.println("Annotated Class:");
                writer.println("Package: " + packageName);
                writer.println("Class: " + className);
                writer.println("Description: " + description);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(Diagnostic.Kind.ERROR, "Failed to generate report: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In this solution, we create a custom annotation processor by extending `AbstractProcessor` and annotating it with `@SupportedAnnotationTypes` and `@SupportedSourceVersion`. The `process` method is overridden to scan for classes annotated with `@CustomAnnotation` and generate a report for each annotated class using the `generateReport` method. The report is written to a file named `report.txt` in the `SOURCE_OUTPUT` location. If any errors occur during report generation, an error message is printed using the `Messager` provided by the processing environment.

