### Problem:
You are tasked with creating a custom annotation processor for a Java application. The application uses the MapStruct library for object mapping and has an interface `SysFileMapper` annotated with `@Mapper`. Your goal is to implement a custom annotation processor that will analyze the `SysFileMapper` interface and generate additional code based on the methods defined within it.

Your custom annotation processor should perform the following tasks:
1. Identify all the methods declared in the `SysFileMapper` interface.
2. For each method, generate additional code to log the method name and its parameters at the beginning of the method execution.
3. The generated code should use the `java.util.logging.Logger` to log the method name and its parameters.

Your task is to implement the custom annotation processor to achieve the described functionality. Assume that the `SysFileMapper` interface extends `BaseMapper<SysFile>`.

### Solution:
```java
import javax.annotation.processing.AbstractProcessor;
import javax.annotation.processing.RoundEnvironment;
import javax.annotation.processing.SupportedAnnotationTypes;
import javax.lang.model.element.Element;
import javax.lang.model.element.ElementKind;
import javax.lang.model.element.ExecutableElement;
import javax.lang.model.element.TypeElement;
import javax.tools.Diagnostic;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.Set;

@SupportedAnnotationTypes("org.mapstruct.Mapper")
public class CustomAnnotationProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {

    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(Mapper.class)) {
            if (element.getKind() == ElementKind.INTERFACE) {
                generateLoggingCode((TypeElement) element);
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private void generateLoggingCode(TypeElement element) {
        String className = element.getSimpleName().toString();
        String loggerName = className + "Logger";
        try (PrintWriter writer = processingEnv.getFiler().createSourceFile(loggerName).openWriter()) {
            writer.println("import java.util.logging.Logger;");
            writer.println("public class " + loggerName + " {");
            writer.println("    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(" + className + ".class.getName());");
            for (Element enclosedElement : element.getEnclosedElements()) {
                if (enclosedElement.getKind() == ElementKind.METHOD) {
                    ExecutableElement method = (ExecutableElement) enclosedElement;
                    String methodName = method.getSimpleName().toString();
                    String parameters = method.getParameters().stream()
                            .map(param -> param.asType() + " " + param.getSimpleName())
                            .reduce((p1, p2) -> p1 + ", " + p2)
                            .orElse("");
                    writer.println("    public void " + methodName + "(" + parameters + ") {");
                    writer.println("        logger.info(\"Executing method " + methodName + " with parameters: " + parameters + "\");");
                    writer.println("    }");
                }
            }
            writer.println("}");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(Diagnostic.Kind.ERROR, "Error generating logging code: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This solution provides a custom annotation processor that processes the `@Mapper` annotation and generates a logging class for the methods in the annotated interface. The generated logging class logs the method name and its parameters using `java.util.logging.Logger`.

