- The program should read a text file named "input.txt" located in the same directory as the program.
- The program should count the occurrences of each word in the file, ignoring case sensitivity (e.g., "Hello" and "hello" should be considered the same word).
- The program should output the word count for each unique word in the file in the format "word: count" sorted alphabetically by the word.

For example, given the input file "input.txt" with the following content:
```
Hello world
This is a hello world
```
The program should output:
```
a: 1
hello: 2
is: 1
this: 1
world: 2
```

### Solution:
```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class WordCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Map<String, Integer> wordCountMap = new TreeMap<>();

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("input.txt"))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                String[] words = line.split("\\s+");
                for (String word : words) {
                    word = word.toLowerCase();
                    wordCountMap.put(word, wordCountMap.getOrDefault(word, 0) + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : wordCountMap.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue());
        }
    }
}
