You are tasked with implementing a custom data structure in C# that mimics the functionality of a stack but also keeps track of the minimum element in the stack at any given time. You need to create a class `MinStack` that supports the following operations:
1. `Push(int x)`: Add element `x` to the top of the stack.
2. `Pop()`: Remove the element on the top of the stack.
3. `Top()`: Retrieve the element on the top of the stack.
4. `GetMin()`: Retrieve the minimum element in the stack.

You need to implement the `MinStack` class using the provided code snippet as a starting point. You can use additional data structures to support the required operations efficiently.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class MinStack
{
    private Stack<int> stack;
    private Stack<int> minStack;

    public MinStack()
    {
        stack = new Stack<int>();
        minStack = new Stack<int>();
    }

    public void Push(int x)
    {
        stack.Push(x);
        if (minStack.Count == 0 || x <= minStack.Peek())
        {
            minStack.Push(x);
        }
    }

    public void Pop()
    {
        if (stack.Count > 0)
        {
            if (stack.Peek() == minStack.Peek())
            {
                minStack.Pop();
            }
            stack.Pop();
        }
    }

    public int Top()
    {
        if (stack.Count > 0)
        {
            return stack.Peek();
        }
        throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
    }

    public int GetMin()
    {
        if (minStack.Count > 0)
        {
            return minStack.Peek();
        }
        throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
    }
}
```

